Big Picture: What This Exercise Is Teaching

This exercise trains you to understand:

Exceptions in C++

Const correctness

Encapsulation (private attributes)

Class interaction

Nested exception classes

Operator overloading

Object responsibility design

You are building a small real-world OOP system:

👨‍💼 Bureaucrat

📄 Form

Bureaucrat signs Form

Exceptions control invalid behavior

This is object interaction + error handling.

🧱 STEP 1 — Understand the Form Class Structure

You must create a class Form.

It has 4 attributes:

1️⃣ const std::string name

Once created → can NEVER change.

So it must be initialized in the constructor initializer list.

2️⃣ bool isSigned

Starts as false

Changes to true if signed

3️⃣ const int gradeToSign

Minimum required grade to sign

4️⃣ const int gradeToExecute

Used later (Exercise 02)

Just store it correctly now

⚠️ All attributes must be:

private

NOT protected.

This teaches:

Proper encapsulation.

🧠 STEP 2 — Grade Rules (Very Important)

Grades follow the SAME RULES as Bureaucrat:

Grade 1 → highest

Grade 150 → lowest

So:

Grade	Meaning
1	strongest
50	mid
150	weakest

That means:

If grade < 1 → ❌ GradeTooHighException
If grade > 150 → ❌ GradeTooLowException

You must check this:

Inside the constructor of Form

Just like you did in Bureaucrat.

🧨 STEP 3 — Nested Exception Classes

Inside Form, you must define:

Form::GradeTooHighException
Form::GradeTooLowException

They must:

Inherit from std::exception

Override what()

Be non-throwing (throw() in C++98)

This teaches:

Polymorphism + custom exceptions

🏗 STEP 4 — Constructor Logic

When creating a Form:

Initialize all const members

Validate grades

If invalid → throw exception

Set isSigned = false

This teaches:

Constructor validation & object integrity

📤 STEP 5 — Getters

You must create getters for:

getName()

getIsSigned()

getGradeToSign()

getGradeToExecute()

Important:

They must be const

They must not modify the object

This reinforces:

Const correctness

🖨 STEP 6 — Overload operator<<

You must print something like:

Form name: Contract
Signed: No
Grade to sign: 50
Grade to execute: 25

This teaches:

Operator overloading
Clean class printing

Remember:

It must not modify the object

It must use getters

🖊 STEP 7 — Implement beSigned()

This is the core logic.

Function:

void beSigned(const Bureaucrat& b);

It must:

Compare bureaucrat grade

If bureaucrat grade <= gradeToSign → sign form

Else → throw GradeTooLowException

⚠️ Remember:
Smaller number = higher power.

So:

If:

bureaucrat.getGrade() <= gradeToSign

→ allowed

Else:
→ exception

This teaches:

Class collaboration
Business logic inside the right class

Important design rule:
Form decides if it can be signed.
Not the Bureaucrat.

🏛 STEP 8 — Modify Bureaucrat Class

Now add:

void signForm(Form& form);

What it does:

Try calling form.beSigned(*this)

If success → print:

Ali signed Contract

If exception → catch it and print:

Ali couldn’t sign Contract because Grade too low

This teaches:

Try/catch usage
Delegation of responsibility
Separation of concerns

Important concept:

Bureaucrat tries

Form decides

Exception explains failure

That’s clean OOP design.

🧪 STEP 9 — What You MUST Test

You must test at least:

✅ Valid form creation

Normal grades

❌ Form grade too high

gradeToSign = 0

❌ Form grade too low

gradeToSign = 151

✅ Bureaucrat signs successfully
❌ Bureaucrat grade too low to sign

Example test scenarios:

Bureaucrat Grade	Form Required	Expected
10	20	❌ fail
20	10	✅ success
1	1	✅ success
150	1	❌ fail
🎯 What This Exercise REALLY Teaches

This is not about forms.

It teaches:

1️⃣ Who owns logic?

Form owns signing rules.

2️⃣ How to design exception flow?

Throw inside low-level class.
Catch inside high-level class.

3️⃣ How to build safe objects?

Validate in constructor.

4️⃣ Why const matters?

Immutable data = safer objects.

⚠️ Common Student Mistakes

❌ Forgetting initializer list for const members
❌ Forgetting to validate grades in constructor
❌ Wrong comparison (grade logic reversed)
❌ Catching exception inside beSigned() (wrong place)
❌ Printing inside Form instead of Bureaucrat
❌ Forgetting const correctness
❌ Making attributes protected

🧠 Mental Model of the System

Think of it like this:

Bureaucrat -----> Form
   |                |
   | tries          | decides
   |                |
   catches <--------

Exception travels upward.

This is called:

Stack unwinding

Which you studied earlier.

🚀 After This Exercise You Should Understand

Nested exception classes

Const data members

Constructor validation

Object collaboration

Proper responsibility design

Real OOP thinking

This exercise prepares you for:

Exercise 02 → Executable forms
Exercise 03 → Inheritance + polymorphism

Which become much more advanced.

🧭 Your Implementation Order (Recommended)

Copy previous ex00

Create Form.hpp

Define attributes

Add constructor + validation

Add nested exceptions

Add getters

Overload operator<<

Implement beSigned()

Modify Bureaucrat with signForm()

Write tests